To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in WordPress when using Next.js, you can add a CORS middleware to your Next.js application. Here's how:

1. First, make sure you have the `cors` package installed in your Next.js project. You can install it using npm or yarn:
```bash
npm install cors
```
or
```bash
yarn add cors
```
2. Next, create a new file called `cors.js` in your `pages/api` directory. This file will contain the CORS middleware.
3. Open the `cors.js` file and add the following code:
```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
  origin: 'http://your-wordpress-site.com', // Replace with the URL of your WordPress site
});

export default cors.createHandler({
  path: '/api/cors',
});
```
Replace `http://your-wordpress-site.com` with the actual URL of your WordPress site.
4. Now, update your `next.config.js` file to include the `cors.js` file as a custom server middleware. Add the following code to the `serverMiddleware` array:
```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  serverMiddleware: [
    // ...
    '/api/cors',
  ],
};
```
5. Finally, test your Next.js application to ensure that the CORS error has been fixed. You should now be able to access your WordPress site from your Next.js application without any CORS errors.

