Sure! Let's consider a simple MATLAB simulation of natural selection. We will model a population of rabbits with two traits: fur color (either white or brown) and speed. The rabbits' fitness will be determined by their ability to survive and reproduce based on these traits.

First, let's define the parameters for our simulation:

```MATLAB
% Population size
N = 100;

% Number of generations
G = 100;

% Probability of survival for each trait
p_survival_white = 0.8;
p_survival_brown = 0.9;

% Probability of reproduction for each trait
p_reproduction_white = 0.7;
p_reproduction_brown = 0.8;

% Mutation rate
mutation_rate = 0.01;
```

Next, we will create a function to simulate the natural selection process:

```MATLAB
function [population, fitness] = natural_selection(population, p_survival, p_reproduction, mutation_rate)
    % Copy the population to avoid modifying the original
    new_population = population;

    % Calculate the fitness of each individual
    fitness = zeros(size(population));
    for i = 1:size(population, 1)
        fitness(i) = p_survival(population(i, 1)) * p_reproduction(population(i, 1));
    end

    % Select individuals for the next generation based on their fitness
    selected_individuals = randperm(size(fitness, 1), N);
    new_population = population(selected_individuals, :);

    % Apply mutation
    mutation_mask = rand(size(new_population, 1), 1) < mutation_rate;
    new_population(mutation_mask, 1) = ~new_population(mutation_mask, 1);

    % Update the population
    population = new_population;
end
```

Now, we can run the simulation for our rabbit population

